From 682109dad4142907d35b79c2d5f5ce4a25a08837 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:35:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 11/19] feat(core,helm): bounded waits on writes and deletes;
 helm_install wait, timeout and rollback_on_failure (C5, K8S-05)

Agents polled after every write: 7 polls (3,548 tokens) on the first
native series, 6 calls on the second, to see a rollout finish, a Pod
become Ready or an object disappear. helm_install had the opposite
problem: a hard-coded 5-minute wait with no rollback (K8S-05), longer
than common client timeouts, so the outcome was lost and the failed
release blocked its name.

Core tools: resources_create_or_update, resources_delete,
resources_scale and resources_rollout take `wait` (seconds, 0-120,
default 0). After the write the server re-reads the object(s) until:
- a Deployment, StatefulSet or DaemonSet rollout is complete
  (observed generation, updated and available replicas);
- a Pod is Ready (or Succeeded), a Job Complete (or Failed);
- any other kind exists; a deleted object is gone.
The result gets one "# wait:" line per object saying whether that state
was reached, with the last state seen. A timeout is reported, not
treated as a failure of the write, which did happen. The maximum (120 s)
stays under common client timeouts; the description says to stay under
the client's own.

helm_install and helm_uninstall take `wait` (default true, as before)
and `timeout` (a Go duration, default 5m), the names upstream #1334
proposes as toolset-level options; helm_install also takes
`rollback_on_failure` (Helm's --atomic): on failure or timeout the
release is uninstalled again, so nothing is left behind and the name is
free. When an install fails and the release record exists, the error
now names the release's state ("failed") and what to do before reusing
the name. Defaults are unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
